<!--
  MCU session-rx buffer-pool config — SLIM / constrained variant for
  nrf51-class (Cortex-M0, 16 KB SRAM total) targets. The SSOT for the
  slim session-link receive slot-pool dimensions selected by the
  `buffer-pool-session-rx-slim` atomic feature (and the
  `preset-cortex-m0-minimal` preset that bundles it). The default
  full-rate variant lives in `session_rx_pool_mcu.scxml` (16 x 1536).

  WHY a second pool profile:
  The default MCU session-rx pool is sized for a full-rate, full-MTU
  data node (slot_count 16 = 200 pps x 1 ms x 2.0 burst absorption;
  slot_size 1536 >= udp_session.mtu_bytes 1472), giving 16 x 1536 =
  24.6 KB. On an nrf51-class part with 16 KB SRAM *total*, that one
  pool cannot fit. Measured (R311jc): the MCU acceptor session e2e's
  peak heap is 37.9 KB, of which ~97% is the two pool-sized sockets
  (session-rx 24.6 KB + reactive-peer 12 KB) and only ~1.3 KB is the
  session engine + HMAC. So the binding cost is the socket pool dims,
  not the session machinery — slimming the pool is what makes the
  stack fit. This constrained profile therefore carries the session
  link as a SMALL-CONTROL-FRAME path, not a full-MTU data path:

    - slot_size 256 — this constrained session carries only the
      unicast handshake + small control frames; the largest is the
      InitAck (3 hdr + 4 zid + 4 sn/batch/cookie_len + 16 cookie =
      27 bytes), so 256 is a ~9x margin. Matches scout_rx_pool_mcu's
      256 control-plane slot_size (same small-frame class). This is
      DELIBERATELY below udp_session.mtu_bytes (1472): a full-MTU data
      datagram would be truncated here. The trade is explicit — this
      profile is for control / handshake-class traffic on ultra-
      constrained SRAM; a node needing full-MTU data uses the default
      session_rx_pool_mcu (and must have the SRAM for it).
    - slot_count 4 — handshake depth (InitSyn / OpenSyn + a 2-fragment
      chain = at most 2 datagrams queued at once) plus headroom, vs
      the default 16 burst-absorption for a 200 pps full-rate node. A
      constrained node is low-rate by construction.

  Net: 4 x 256 = 1 KB rx pool (vs 24.6 KB). With a matching slim
  reactive-peer socket the e2e peak heap drops to ~3.5 KB, fitting the
  nrf51 16 KB budget (lwIP static ~8 KB + stack ~3 KB + heap ~4 KB ~=
  15 KB) — graduating the Layer Q.4 microbit acceptor lane from
  build-only to a real on-target boot.

  cache-policy none + section sram1 + alignment 32 mirror
  session_rx_pool_mcu (same live consumer: LwipUdpSocket copies each
  datagram via pbuf_copy_partial into an inline heapless Vec, not the
  §5.E DMA slot table, so no DMA cache maintenance).

  Consumed by: wz-link-lwip::rx_sockets::SessionRxSocket when the
  `buffer-pool-session-rx-slim` feature is active (the link tier
  selects this pool's SLOT_COUNT / SLOT_SIZE over the default). The
  live deploy topology that selects it is the `preset-cortex-m0-
  minimal` Cargo preset; a full deploy/*.yaml constrained topology
  that `{ ref: session_rx_pool_mcu_minimal }`s this pool (the
  topology-doc twin of deploy/mcu_target.yaml's default-pool ref)
  follows the R50 deploy-topology carry, not codegen-live today.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="buffer-pool" name="session_rx_pool_mcu_minimal" version="1.0">
  <sce:slot-count>4</sce:slot-count>
  <sce:slot-size>256</sce:slot-size>
  <sce:section>sram1</sce:section>
  <sce:alignment>32</sce:alignment>
  <sce:cache-policy>none</sce:cache-policy>
</scxml>
